1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:05,110
Have a look at this picture. It was
printed in Paris, in the summer of 1898.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,400
That's an African emperor
in a red cape, riding

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,570
through the rocks at a place called Adwa.

4
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:14,820
Adwa is a town in the
mountains of northern Ethiopia,

5
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:17,250
not far from today's border with Eritrea.

6
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:22,040
This is a later artistic
reconstruction. But the battle was real.

7
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:29,010
On 1 March, 1896, Ethiopia destroyed an
invading Italian army in a single day.

8
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:33,570
If you know this story at all,
you probably know how it ends.

9
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,130
The real question is how they did it.

10
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:40,910
This is the story of Adwa.

11
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:47,367
It starts seven years earlier, in 1889,

12
00:00:47,407 --> 00:00:50,370
with a new emperor and
a treaty with Italy.

13
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:54,382
The emperor was Menelik II.
The treaty was named

14
00:00:54,422 --> 00:00:57,090
after the place it was signed: Wuchale.

15
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:03,310
Most of it was about friendship,
trade and borders. One article wasn't.

16
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:07,279
Article 17 was written in
two languages. And here's the

17
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:10,370
problem: the two versions
didn't say the same thing.

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:14,380
In the Amharic version, the
emperor could use Italy's

19
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:17,410
help in dealing with other
powers, if he wanted to.

20
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,660
In the Italian version, he
agreed to do so. Read that

21
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,930
way, Ethiopia had become
an Italian protectorate.

22
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,290
So it comes down to
one verb. May, or must.

23
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,060
Italy told the rest of
Europe that it now spoke

24
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,130
for Ethiopia. Menelik
wouldn't accept that.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,740
Historians still argue about
whether that was a translator's

26
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:43,390
mistake, or done on purpose.
I don't think we can be sure.

27
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:48,010
In 1893, Menelik formally
rejected the treaty.

28
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,879
To follow what happened
next, you need to meet the

29
00:01:51,919 --> 00:01:54,090
two people at the centre
of the Ethiopian court.

30
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:57,100
Menelik had already ruled
the kingdom of Shewa for

31
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,450
more than twenty years
before he became emperor.

32
00:02:00,123 --> 00:02:03,951
He was patient, and he planned
ahead. Through the 1880s and 1890s,

33
00:02:03,991 --> 00:02:06,782
he imported modern
rifles in large numbers.

34
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:10,040
Next to him was the empress, Taytu Betul.

35
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:12,190
And she wasn't just in the background.

36
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,220
She was a political
force in her own right.

37
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:18,059
Together, in the 1880s,
they founded a new capital

38
00:02:18,099 --> 00:02:21,200
and called it Addis Ababa.
It means new flower.

39
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:24,770
By one account, it was Taytu who
argued hardest against Italy's

40
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:27,580
reading of the treaty, and
pressed her husband to stand firm.

41
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:31,380
But there's a harder side to
this story too, and it matters.

42
00:02:32,135 --> 00:02:35,787
In the same years, Menelik's armies were
conquering kingdoms to the south and

43
00:02:35,827 --> 00:02:40,803
west, like Wolaita. Kaffa followed
in eighteen ninety-seven, after Adwa.

44
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:45,110
A lot of those campaigns were
brutal. For many people in Ethiopia's

45
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:48,500
south, Menelik is remembered as
a conqueror, not a liberator.

46
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:51,510
Both of those things are
true. The empire that fought

47
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:54,020
at Adwa had itself been built by force.

48
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:58,330
Over in Rome, the prime
minister, Francesco Crispi,

49
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:01,460
wanted an empire. The treaty
row gave him his excuse.

50
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:06,160
Italy already had a colony on the
Red Sea coast. It called it Eritrea.

51
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:10,634
In 1895, Italian forces under
General Oreste Baratieri pushed

52
00:03:10,674 --> 00:03:14,220
south from there, into the
Ethiopian province of Tigray.

53
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:16,840
Menelik answered in September.

54
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:19,440
He called the whole empire to arms.

55
00:03:20,170 --> 00:03:22,250
And this came after years
of famine and cattle

56
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:24,960
disease. Even so, the provinces answered.

57
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:29,170
From Shewa and Gojjam, from
Tigray and the Oromo lands

58
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:32,300
of Wollo, forces gathered
under their own commanders.

59
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:36,494
How big was the army?
Honestly, nobody knows exactly.

60
00:03:36,534 --> 00:03:40,440
Estimates run from
about 70,000 men to more than 100,000.

61
00:03:41,210 --> 00:03:43,240
And an army that size has to eat.

62
00:03:44,330 --> 00:03:48,212
Behind the fighters came pack animals,
grain and thousands of camp followers.

63
00:03:48,252 --> 00:03:51,720
Just moving all of that north
was a huge piece of organisation.

64
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:56,030
According to the accounts,
Taytu marched with

65
00:03:56,070 --> 00:03:57,960
them, at the head of her own troops.

66
00:03:59,882 --> 00:04:05,170
A major Ethiopian victory came on
7 December, at a mountain called Amba Alagi.

67
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:09,803
Ethiopian forces under Ras
Makonnen overran an Italian outpost

68
00:04:09,843 --> 00:04:13,660
of around 2,000 men. Most of
them were Eritrean soldiers.

69
00:04:14,410 --> 00:04:17,260
Their commander, Major
Pietro Toselli, was killed.

70
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:22,300
The survivors fell back to a fort the
Italians had started building at Mekelle.

71
00:04:23,289 --> 00:04:26,099
In January, the Ethiopian
army surrounded it.

72
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:30,600
The fort had one weak spot. Its
water was outside the walls.

73
00:04:31,797 --> 00:04:36,727
So Ethiopian troops took the springs.
Some accounts credit Taytu with the plan.

74
00:04:37,452 --> 00:04:40,542
Within about two weeks, the
garrison put up a white flag.

75
00:04:41,372 --> 00:04:43,492
Then Menelik did something
you might not expect.

76
00:04:43,532 --> 00:04:45,562
He let the Italian soldiers march away.

77
00:04:46,531 --> 00:04:48,712
Why he did it is still
debated. But it was the

78
00:04:48,752 --> 00:04:51,002
move of a ruler thinking
beyond one battle.

79
00:04:52,911 --> 00:04:56,941
By February, the two armies were facing
each other in the mountains around Adwa.

80
00:04:57,971 --> 00:05:01,120
Neither commander wanted to
attack first across that ground.

81
00:05:01,811 --> 00:05:03,841
And both armies were running out of food.

82
00:05:04,590 --> 00:05:07,370
Baratieri thought that if he
waited, Menelik's army would

83
00:05:07,410 --> 00:05:10,121
have to break up and
scatter just to feed itself.

84
00:05:10,991 --> 00:05:12,581
But Rome wasn't going to wait.

85
00:05:13,651 --> 00:05:16,921
Crispi's telegrams were getting
sharper. He wanted a victory.

86
00:05:17,731 --> 00:05:19,851
And by most accounts, a
general was already on

87
00:05:19,891 --> 00:05:22,421
his way from Italy to replace Baratieri.

88
00:05:23,238 --> 00:05:26,100
In late February, Baratieri
called in his brigade commanders

89
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:28,817
and put it to them: pull back, or advance?

90
00:05:29,771 --> 00:05:31,981
By most accounts, they
told him to advance.

91
00:05:33,851 --> 00:05:35,881
The plan they came up with was bold.

92
00:05:36,631 --> 00:05:39,431
Three brigades would march through
the night on separate tracks,

93
00:05:39,471 --> 00:05:43,261
and take a line of high ground facing
the Ethiopian camp before dawn.

94
00:05:45,131 --> 00:05:48,051
On the left was General Matteo
Albertone, with a brigade

95
00:05:48,091 --> 00:05:50,861
of Eritrean soldiers
under Italian officers.

96
00:05:51,871 --> 00:05:55,908
General Giuseppe Arimondi had the
centre, and General Vittorio Dabormida

97
00:05:55,948 --> 00:05:59,381
the right. A fourth brigade
followed behind, in reserve.

98
00:06:01,451 --> 00:06:04,941
Altogether, somewhere between
15,000 and 18,000 men.

99
00:06:05,871 --> 00:06:09,541
They set off at about half past
nine at night, on 29 February.

100
00:06:10,431 --> 00:06:13,541
It was dark. The tracks were
rough. And their maps were poor.

101
00:06:14,371 --> 00:06:17,981
On paper, it was a plan. In the
mountains, it started to fall apart.

102
00:06:19,911 --> 00:06:22,421
Albertone marched further
than he was meant to.

103
00:06:23,411 --> 00:06:25,831
By most accounts, his guides
told him the hill he'd

104
00:06:25,871 --> 00:06:28,461
reached wasn't the one
he'd been ordered to hold.

105
00:06:30,211 --> 00:06:33,321
So he kept going, kilometres
ahead of the rest of the army.

106
00:06:34,958 --> 00:06:39,179
Around dawn, his brigade ran into
the Ethiopian army. On its own.

107
00:06:40,191 --> 00:06:43,731
His Eritrean soldiers fought
hard. But a gap had opened up

108
00:06:43,771 --> 00:06:46,601
behind them, and Menelik's
commanders poured into it.

109
00:06:47,631 --> 00:06:50,441
Baratieri ordered
Dabormida forward to help.

110
00:06:51,491 --> 00:06:54,831
Instead, Dabormida's brigade
drifted off to the right,

111
00:06:54,871 --> 00:06:58,381
down into a valley, and into
a separate fight of its own.

112
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:02,521
Now the Italian army was in pieces.

113
00:07:04,191 --> 00:07:06,961
Three brigades. Three battles. No line.

114
00:07:08,911 --> 00:07:14,061
In the centre, Arimondi's men held on
for a while, against charge after charge.

115
00:07:15,407 --> 00:07:17,417
Then Menelik sent in his reserve.

116
00:07:18,527 --> 00:07:21,307
According to the accounts,
Ras Mikael's Oromo

117
00:07:21,347 --> 00:07:23,917
cavalry came down on the Italian flanks.

118
00:07:24,787 --> 00:07:26,037
And the centre broke.

119
00:07:26,842 --> 00:07:30,633
The Italian centre had collapsed, but
fighting went on into the afternoon.

120
00:07:30,766 --> 00:07:34,650
Arimondi and Dabormida were killed,
and Albertone was taken prisoner.

121
00:07:35,477 --> 00:07:39,327
Baratieri was retreating, with
whatever was left of his army.

122
00:07:41,113 --> 00:07:42,872
Estimates of the losses differ.

123
00:07:43,573 --> 00:07:46,598
By one estimate, the Italian-led
army lost about 6,000

124
00:07:46,638 --> 00:07:49,674
men killed, and around 3,000
were taken prisoner.

125
00:07:50,497 --> 00:07:54,147
A lot of those men were Eritrean
soldiers in Italian service.

126
00:07:54,337 --> 00:07:57,707
It's worth remembering that
Africans died on both sides at Adwa.

127
00:07:58,877 --> 00:08:04,567
Ethiopian losses were heavy too. Estimates
run from about 4,000 to 7,000 dead.

128
00:08:05,637 --> 00:08:09,647
A European army had been beaten in
open battle by an African state.

129
00:08:11,757 --> 00:08:14,257
In London, The Graphic printed this scene,

130
00:08:14,297 --> 00:08:16,667
drawn from details given by survivors.

131
00:08:17,377 --> 00:08:19,697
In Italy, people protested in the streets.

132
00:08:19,737 --> 00:08:22,287
Within days, Crispi's
government had fallen.

133
00:08:24,258 --> 00:08:26,228
Menelik didn't march on to the coast.

134
00:08:26,978 --> 00:08:30,147
He was holding thousands of prisoners,
and he used them to bargain.

135
00:08:31,378 --> 00:08:35,947
In October 1896, the Treaty
of Addis Ababa ended the war.

136
00:08:36,938 --> 00:08:39,766
Italy cancelled the Treaty
of Wuchale and recognised

137
00:08:39,806 --> 00:08:42,168
Ethiopia as a fully independent state.

138
00:08:42,921 --> 00:08:46,654
In eighteen ninety-seven, France and
Britain signed agreements with Menelik.

139
00:08:47,368 --> 00:08:51,498
But Italy kept Eritrea. Adwa
didn't push Europe out of Africa.

140
00:08:52,428 --> 00:08:56,748
What it did was narrower, but still
huge. It kept Ethiopia independent

141
00:08:56,788 --> 00:08:59,758
while almost all the rest of the
continent was being carved up.

142
00:09:01,688 --> 00:09:05,078
For a long time, Europe told
Adwa as a story about Italy.

143
00:09:05,808 --> 00:09:09,118
A disaster. A blunder.
Generals lost in the dark.

144
00:09:09,988 --> 00:09:12,378
Tell it that way, and the
winners almost vanish.

145
00:09:14,028 --> 00:09:15,778
Ethiopians told it differently.

146
00:09:16,588 --> 00:09:19,268
In paintings like this one,
made long after the battle,

147
00:09:19,308 --> 00:09:22,278
Saint George rides in the
sky above the Ethiopian army.

148
00:09:23,008 --> 00:09:26,938
But the victory belongs to the people
who planned it, fed it, and fought it.

149
00:09:27,688 --> 00:09:31,488
Far beyond Ethiopia, Black writers
and activists, in Africa and across

150
00:09:31,528 --> 00:09:35,238
the Atlantic, saw Adwa as proof
that colonial rule could be beaten.

151
00:09:36,038 --> 00:09:39,028
And the independence Adwa
protected didn't last forever.

152
00:09:40,058 --> 00:09:42,868
In 1935, Italy invaded
again, under Mussolini,

153
00:09:42,908 --> 00:09:45,888
and occupied much of
the country until 1941.

154
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:50,422
Today, Ethiopia commemorates
the victory with the Adwa

155
00:09:50,462 --> 00:09:53,150
Victory Day holiday,
on the second of March.

156
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:57,670
So, let's go back to that
picture printed in Paris.

157
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,010
How did Ethiopia win?

158
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:03,450
Not because of one hero. And not by luck.

159
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:07,540
It won because an emperor
armed his state ahead of time,

160
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:09,870
and an empress refused
to accept the treaty.

161
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:15,410
Because the provinces answered the call,
and the army was fed, moved and led.

162
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:18,700
And because the invader
marched into the dark,

163
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:21,630
on poor maps, with Rome
demanding a victory.

164
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:26,250
Ethiopia won at Adwa because
it was ready, and Italy wasn't.

165
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:30,870
This has been OrigineX Human Archives.

166
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:35,430
If you think this record is
worth keeping, please subscribe.
